From 9034e99e50aefc46ae18dfdb71485cd69c599633 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jun Kawasaki <jun784@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 01:50:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sealer: brand pairs, so provenance stops being an identity
 question
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A large share of the places that look like they need identity are not asking
who are you. They are asking did this come back from me — is this the
capability I minted, the ticket I issued. An identity answer needs a
principal, a proof, a registry and a comparison. A brand answers it with
object identity and nothing else, and it is pure: sealing performs no effect,
consults nothing, reaches nothing outside its arguments. A decision that
resolves an identifier becomes an effect, which ADR-2608160200's Execution CID
memo rule forbids; a brand has no such cost, so the cases it covers should be
spent here rather than on identity.

`brand-pair` returns the two rights separately because the split is the
mechanism: handing out the sealer confers the right to put values into the
brand and confers nothing else. Two pairs made with the same label are
unrelated — the label is for receipts, is never compared, and a brand is not
its name. `unseal` returns `{:ok? false :reason ...}` rather than throwing,
per the `:explicit-errors` invariant, over a closed reason set that keeps
`:not-sealed` (a hand-built look-alike) distinct from `:wrong-brand` (a real
box from elsewhere).

An earlier draft of the docstring claimed no public constructor could widen a
sealer into an unsealer. The test written to demonstrate that failed:
`deftype` emits a public `->Unsealer` on every platform and `^:private` on the
type does not suppress it. The claim is now the narrower true one — nothing
this namespace exports hands out a brand core — and the interop surface is
named in a contract test so that adding a widening function here reddens
something instead of reddening nothing.

Measured: 9 tests / 35 assertions / 0 failures. Full suite 249 -> 258 tests,
1364 -> 1399 assertions, with the identical 5 failures / 3 errors before and
after (all of them sibling checkouts this environment does not have —
`../kotoba/...` — which is the drift the `:ocapn-test` alias comment already
describes). `clojure -M:lint` 0 errors, and the one warning this change
introduced is fixed rather than accepted.

Each behaviour was shown to discriminate: comparing labels instead of brand
identity reddens 2 tests, letting `unseal` accept a sealer reddens 1, and
collapsing `:not-sealed` into `:wrong-brand` reddens 3.

